  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 AM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThe area remains in a northwest flow aloft today with a weak \nboundary near the area. This boundary will begin to lift north \ntoday and may be a focus for widely scattered rain chances this \nafternoon. Better rain chances, at least for the eastern half of\nthe area, arrive on Tuesday. A strong cold front will move \nthrough the Quad State region and will be the focus for showers \nand storms. A very moist airmass ahead of the front will \ninteract with increasing shear from a jet to the north for the \npossibility for strong to severe storms. The greater threat \nremains to our east but there is enough overlap of \nshear/instability for damaging winds and hail in any stronger\nstorms. Timing continues to favor the afternoon hours but it is\nnoted that early look at the CAMs do suggest activity moving \ninto the region as early as Tuesday morning. \n\nBehind the front, high pressure will offer much drier and \ncooler conditions Wednesday into Thursday. Another upper \ndisturbance moving through the region may offer more rain \nchances late Friday into Friday night. Most of the weekend looks\ndry although a few isolated showers/storms are possible on the \neastern periphery of a ridge located across the plains. Hot and\nhumid conditions will continue early this week. Tuesday will be\nthe warmest day when highs push well into the 90s. These \ntemperatures along with dewpoints in the 70s will result in heat\nindex readings approaching or possibly exceeding 110 degrees. A\nheat advisory remains in effect for portions of the area \nthrough Tuesday evening. Depending on coverage/timing of \nconvection, upgraded headlines are possible on Tuesday. \nTemperatures cool into the 80s Wednesday and into the weekend.",
